1	//! End-to-end proof that an AWL `retry N` declaration buys N FURTHER attempts.
2	//!
3	//! The ratified meaning (`docs/design/aion-authoring/awl/`
4	//! `AWL-COMPLETION-COLD-READ.md` §2.3, and the `RetryContract` doc comments in
5	//! `aion-package`) is that `retry N` declares N further attempts AFTER the
6	//! first — N+1 total executions. The engine's dispatch loop is convention-free
7	//! (`attempt < max_attempts`), so the meaning is owned entirely by the two
8	//! places a declared count becomes the SDK's total-attempt budget: the
9	//! reference emitter and the direct MIR lowering.
10	//!
11	//! This gate runs ONE `retry 3` workflow through BOTH backends against a real
12	//! embedded engine and an always-failing dispatcher, and counts deliveries.
13	//! Four executions — the first plus three further — is the contract; anything
14	//! else is the engine disagreeing with its own documentation, or the two
15	//! backends disagreeing with each other.
16	//!
17	//! Both packages share the SDK closure and differ only in the entry module's
18	//! bytes (the BC-4 splice discipline), so a divergence can only be a backend
19	//! difference.
